    body: ["Long before tarot decks were sold in bookshops, people read the deck they already had. Playing cards reached Europe in the fourteenth century, and within a few generations readers were laying them out on kitchen tables to ask about love, money, journeys and trouble. The first systematic method was printed in Paris in 1770 by Jean-Baptiste Alliette, who wrote under the name Etteilla and read with the 32-card piquet deck, the deck French card games used. The tradition that grew from his work still reads 32 cards in France today.", "English and American readers kept the full deck of 52. Their suit meanings also settled differently. In most English-language books Diamonds are money and the material world, Clubs are work, growth and friendship, Hearts are love and the home, and Spades are difficulty and truth. In the French lineage Clubs (trefle) often carry money and luck, and Diamonds (carreau) carry news and journeys. Neither is more correct; they are two dialects. Arcana speaks the English and American one, because it is the one most of the sources in English agree on, and we say so whenever a card's meaning differs between the two.", "What you will not find on a playing card is a picture telling you what to feel. That is the strength of the deck. You read the suit for the subject, the number for the stage, and the color for the mood, and then you read the cards next to each other. The rest of these lessons teach you exactly that, one layer at a time."],
